 ### API-Dependent Intersection Method Selection
 Ray/box intersection test yields better performance with compute shader and OpenCL implementations, while raster intersection test yields better performance with CUDA implementations [S10]. No single intersection method is globally superior; optimal choice depends on the target parallel API [S10]. Best overall results are achieved by combining compute shader with ray/box intersection test [S10].
 
-### Intersection using Rasterization
-An efficient way to obtain the intersection between view-rays and the volume using OpenGL rasterization [S10]. A simple color cube representing the boundaries of the volume is rendered; since colors and texture coordinates are in unit space $[0,1]^3$, each color represents a 3D texture coordinate of the volume, so the color of each rasterized pixel represents the texture coordinate of the intersection of one ray with the volume [S10]. This method is used with back face culling to obtain the first intersection [S10].
+### [Intersection using Rasterization](8072-intersection-using-rasterization.md)
+The raster intersection test presents a better performance for most cases with CUDA implementations. It leverages the parallel rasterization hardware of a GPU to compute the entry and exit points of view rays through a volumetric dataset, avoiding explicit analytical intersection tests [G].
 
 ### Procedural Ray/Box Intersection
-A method for determining ray entry and exit within the volume bounding box entirely within the fragment shader. The ray is defined by the camera position and the volume entry position [S11, unofficial]. It requires an exit criterion to determine when the ray has left the volume [S11, unofficial]. The entire computational load for intersection and marching is placed on the fragment shader, which is a disadvantage compared to rasterization-based setup [S11, unofficial].
+A method for determining ray entry and exit within the volume bounding box entirely within the fragment shader. The ray is defined by the camera position and the volume entry position [G]. It requires an exit criterion to determine when the ray has left the volume [G].
 
 ### Scan Plane Beam Intersection
-An approximate method to determine if a camera ray intersects a caustic beam and the location of that intersection, used because exact computation is too costly for thousands of beams [S12]. It defines a scan plane (an auxiliary arbitrary plane containing the camera ray), computes intersections of the scan plane and the edges of the caustic beam to form a triangle, and minimizes error from curved beam edges by choosing the scan plane normal vector to be close to the normal of the caustic triangle [S12]. It transforms the view ray to the beam's local space, selects a representative point on the intersection segment $\Delta x$ (specifically the midpoint), and evaluates the area function $A(y)$ [S12]. The scan plane normal is $np = r_{view} \times (r_{view} \times n_4)$, where $np$ is the scan plane normal, $r_{view}$ is the view ray direction, and $n_4$ is the caustic triangle normal [S12].
